WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:21.130
He’s perhaps the greatest ever humanitarian.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.700
Oskar Schindler was a German industrialist
who saved the lives of over 1,000 Jews during

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.700
the Holocaust.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.540
The subject of a hit 1982 book, and a 1993
Steven Spielberg film that hit even bigger,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.000
Oskar Schindler today is a household name.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.640
You probably think you already know him.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.540
How he started out as a cynical businessman
before finding redemption.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.650
How he courted the Nazi leadership to save
Jewish lives.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.240
Even how his humanity was finally stirred
by the sight of one red coated girl dying

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.770
in the Krakow ghetto.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.760
But what if we told you there was far more
to Oskar Schindler than you thought?

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.910
What if we told you he wasn’t just a cynical
businessman, but a war profiteer, a Nazi spy,

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.940
and a traitor who was nearly executed for
collaborating?

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:05.309
In the video today, we’re going to take
you inside the life of Oskar Schindler, and

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:11.020
explore how a one-time Nazi stooge succeeded
in finding his humanity… and, in doing so,

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.020
saved the world.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:24.479
Brave New World

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.810
The story of Oskar Schindler technically begins
on April 28, 1908, when he was born in the

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.579
pretty town of Zwittau.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:34.630
We say “technically”, because there’s
really not much to say about those early days.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.639
Schindler was born into a middle class German
family, had a younger sister, and went to

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:39.639
school.

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:40.649
Exciting stuff, huh?

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.659
From a purely narrative point of view, the
story of Oskar Schindler can actually be said

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:49.189
to start nearly 11 years later, on October
29, 1918.

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:53.490
That’s the date the Schindler family’s
comfortable, middle class world exploded.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.119
See, the Schindlers were ethnic Germans, but
they didn’t live in Germany.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:02.899
They lived in the Austro-Hungarian Empire,
specifically a region known as the Sudetenland.

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:04.709
Above them lay Germany proper.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:07.000
Somewhere below them lay Austria itself.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.099
But between the Schindlers and Austria lay
a land that was very un-German.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:15.599
A land that had spent centuries chafing under
Austrian rule until it was completely sick

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:16.790
of Germans.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.830
That land was about to declare itself the
brand new country of Czechoslovakia.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.450
On that day, October 29, 1918, just as WWI
was drawing to an end, the Czechs and Slovaks

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.510
deposed the Austrians and announced a new,
Slavic republic.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:35.069
Like all Sudeten Germans, the Schindlers took
one look at the emerging Czechoslovak state

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:38.530
and said, “Yeah, we’d rather be with Germany.”

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:39.780
To which the Czechs replied…

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.870
Well, they didn’t actually say anything.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.580
They just picked up their guns and started
shooting.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.490
The pro-German riots that broke out across
the Sudetenland only ended when Czech authorities

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.450
in Kadaň emptied a machinegun into a crowd,
killing 25.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:57.620
In the context of the time, the Kadaň Massacre
wasn’t particularly egregious.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.189
Along the ethnic fault lines of the old Austro-Hungarian
Empire, Poles were killing Czechs, who were

00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:05.510
killing Germans, who were killing Poles, and
so on.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.799
But it did provide a shock to any Sudeten
Germans tempted to buy into this Czechoslovakia

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:11.799
thing.

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:14.360
Like all their neighbors, the Schindlers would
remember Kadaň.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.010
That September, the Treaty of Saint-Germaine
made the Czech takeover official.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:22.829
While this was sucky news for the Sudeten
Germans, we need to be clear that this wasn’t

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:23.890
the apocalypse.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.900
As part of Czechoslovakia, Sudeten Germans
were allowed to continue speaking German.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.830
They could attend German-language schools,
keep their businesses, and so on.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:35.280
An evil occupying force the Czechoslovaks
were not.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.840
But they were resented.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.730
The new Czechoslovakia was 50% Czech, nearly
23% German, and 16% Slovak, plus a handful

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.459
of Jews, Ukrainians, and Hungarians.

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:50.329
But while the Slovaks got their own federal
parliament, the more-numerous Sudeten Germans

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:53.049
were stuck as bit players in the Czech system.

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:54.909
It wasn’t terrible, in other words.

00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:58.760
Some families, such as the Schindlers, even
thrived under the new regime.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.420
But it was a cause of discontent.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.730
You can imagine it as the world’s slowest
timebomb.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.540
By ignoring the Sudeten Germans’ wishes,
the Czechs had just accidently pressed the

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.540
‘on’ button.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.030
That Sudeten timebomb might not explode for
decades.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:18.579
But, when it did, it was going to blow the
whole of Europe to pieces.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:25.880
Marriage and Misery
Right, so that’s the world Oskar Schindler

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:26.880
inhabited.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.100
Now, what about the man himself?

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.120
Well… that’s the problem.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.580
Schindler wasn’t exactly a man yet, so much
as a boy.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.910
A boy who enjoyed nothing more than goofing
off and chasing pretty girls.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.110
And it was going to be a long time before
that boy grew up.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.400
In 1924, Schindler was dismissed from school
for falsifying his report card.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.419
Rather than try to continue his education,
he instead moved into a series of odd jobs

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:51.500
around Zwittau.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.190
The actual content of those jobs doesn’t
matter.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:59.139
They were really just a front for the tall
and charming Schindler to prowl around, seducing

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:01.000
as many girls as he could.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.170
Before he was even 20, Schindler had a reputation
as a guy who loved ladies, loved cars, and

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.210
hated personal responsibility.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.430
Still, even the biggest Casanova will eventually
settle down, and for Schindler that settling

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:13.750
came in 1928.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.479
In late January that year, Schindler traveled
to Alt Moletein for his work as a salesman.

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:22.800
While at the house of a client, he happened
to bump into their daughter.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.389
From the moment he met Emilie Pelzl, Schindler
was knocked for six.

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:30.919
That’s a cricket metaphor; I guess all you
Americans out there would say “knocked for

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:33.630
a homerun” or something equally ludicrous.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:37.300
For her part, Emilie was knocked for a winning
basket, too.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:42.260
The pair began an intense courtship that climaxed
with them marrying just six weeks later, on

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.230
March 6, 1928.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:48.661
As a gift for the married couple, Emilie’s
father gave them 100,000 Czech crowns, perhaps

00:05:48.661 --> 00:05:51.199
hoping they’d buy a little house or something.

00:05:51.199 --> 00:05:55.290
Instead, Schindler blew it all on a luxury
car and the couple moved in above Schindler’s

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:56.290
parents.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.229
For Emilie, the experience was a rude awakening.

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:02.610
Although he claimed to genuinely love her,
Schindler treated his new wife less like a

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.240
lifelong companion, and more like someone
to come home to when he couldn’t find a

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.240
better offer.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:11.009
It was probably a relief when Schindler was
conscripted into the Czechoslovak army for

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:12.890
18 months service.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:17.430
Speaking of Czechoslovakia, things seemed
to be going kind of OK in 1928.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:21.460
The country had existed for ten years now,
and the Sudeten Germans’ initial resistance

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.681
had settled down into a kind of grumbling,
probably helped by the fact the Czechoslovak

00:06:26.681 --> 00:06:31.100
economy was booming while Germany was a Weimar
basket case.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.610
But those resentments were still there.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:39.060
And it would only take one sharp outside shock
to crack them wide open.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.520
That shock came on October 29, 1929.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.190
That day, the Wall Street stock market came
crashing down under the weight of a billion

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:47.419
broken dreams.

00:06:47.419 --> 00:06:49.810
The global economy ground to a halt.

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.099
Hunger, misery, and fear bit.

00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:57.010
For Oskar Schindler, this meant being discharged
from the army just in time to see his old

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.379
company file for bankruptcy.

00:06:58.379 --> 00:07:03.500
Used to a lavish lifestyle, Schindler first
tried moving to Berlin, then opening a poultry

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.289
farm, but nothing worked.

00:07:05.289 --> 00:07:10.319
Finally, he took over his father’s business
in Zwittau, only to watch as it, too, went

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:11.319
bankrupt.

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:12.520
It was a grim and desperate time.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.220
For the Schindlers, for most of the world.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:16.870
But not everyone.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:20.800
Across the Czechoslovak border, a funny little
man with a Charlie Chaplin moustache was about

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.970
to find the Great Depression had created a
willing audience for his message of white

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.530
nationalism and anti-elitism.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.270
His name was Adolf Hitler.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:40.160
And he was about to force every Sudeten German
to make a terrible choice.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.540
The Dictator and the Spy
The suddenness of Hitler’s rise to power

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.949
is the sort of career the word “meteoric”
is reserved for.

00:07:44.949 --> 00:07:50.509
In the 1928 German election, the Nazis won
a paltry 2.5% of all votes, the sort of number

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:54.289
even the US Libertarian Party would laugh
at.

00:07:54.289 --> 00:07:59.080
By the July 1932 election, though, they were
the biggest party with nearly 40% of the vote.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.670
Less than a year later, they would seize power.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.479
Back in the Sudetenland, unemployed and impoverished
Germans watched the rise of the Nazis with

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:08.050
ill-disguised envy.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:14.009
In 1933, a Sudeten German gymnastics coach
named Konrad Henlein decided to try and capture

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:19.169
some of that fascist magic by founding his
own party, the Sudetendeutsche Heimatfront.

00:08:19.169 --> 00:08:23.760
A Nazi organization in all but name, the Heimatfront
had one goal.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.451
To split the Sudetenland off from Czechoslovakia
and unite with Hitler’s Germany.

00:08:27.451 --> 00:08:32.200
It was a goal most of the Sudeten Germans
were more than onboard with.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:38.000
In May, 1935, the Czechoslovak elections returned
44 seats for Henlein’s wannabee Nazis, a

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.289
single seat less than the largest party.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:43.640
That same year, Oskar Schindler officially
joined the Heimatfront.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:47.630
Many biographies of Schindler go to great
lengths to explain that he was a pure opportunist,

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:50.890
that he merely joined for the financial opportunities.

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:54.670
Given what we know of his later life and good
deeds, it’s tempting to agree.

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:55.670
Honestly, we’d love nothing more than to
give you an uncomplicated history of a good

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:56.670
man.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.780
But there’s no escaping the fact that history
is rarely black and white.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:04.430
And had you glanced at Oskar Schindler’s
CV in 1936, you’d have had a hard time believing

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:05.920
he was anything but a Nazi.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.930
That’s the year Schindler joined the Abwehr,
the German intelligence agency.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.550
Under the Abwehr, Schindler turned spy for
Nazi Germany.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:14.920
He began collecting information.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:19.880
Before long, he was working on clandestine
activities that could have only pointed in

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:20.880
one direction.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.339
The Nazis were preparing for an invasion of
Czechoslovakia.

00:09:24.339 --> 00:09:27.760
Schindler would later say that he only took
this work for the money, because he was badly

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:28.760
in debt and addicted to drink.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:29.760
There’s no doubt this is true.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.920
There’s also no doubt that the work of Schindler
and other Sudeten German spies like him helped

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:35.850
Hitler get exactly what he wanted.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:41.589
In February, 1938, Hitler felt confident enough
to demand self-determination for all Sudeten

00:09:41.589 --> 00:09:42.589
Germans.

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:44.380
A month later, he annexed Austria.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:50.070
On April 24, Konrad Henlein – the Nazi gymnast
who set up the Heimatfront – demanded full

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.279
autonomy for the Sudetenland within Czechoslovakia.

00:09:53.279 --> 00:09:58.089
The Czechs told him to take a hike, but Henlein
was merely playing for time.

00:09:58.089 --> 00:10:02.490
On the ground, Schindler and his other spies
fanned out, working overtime to prepare for

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:03.490
the Nazi invasion.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.420
But Oskar Schindler wouldn’t be around to
see it.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.370
On July 18, 1938, Czech authorities arrested
Schindler as a spy.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.529
He was found guilty and sentenced to hang
as a traitor.

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:16.440
He was saved, of all people, by Adolf Hitler.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:21.390
On September 28, Britain, France, Germany
and Italy all met in Munich to hammer out

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:25.560
an agreement that would stop the Sudeten Crisis
from triggering WWII.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.120
The deal they reached was to just give Hitler
everything.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.790
On October 1, the Sudetenland was handed over
to Germany.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.680
At the time Schindler was on death row.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.110
We’ve seen at least a couple of sources
claim he was days or only hours away from

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.500
being executed when the Nazis marched in.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.010
As part of the Munich Agreement, Schindler
was freed.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.160
The traitor had escaped death by the skin
of his teeth.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.089
One month later, Schindler was a citizen of
Germany, living inside the new Sudetenland.

00:10:50.089 --> 00:10:54.560
Perhaps to celebrate, he joined the Nazi Party.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:01.810
Winter for Poland
If you only know Oskar Schindler from Schindler’s

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:05.860
List, you might be wondering when his life
story is gonna start sounding less like one

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:10.390
of our depressing videos about Nazi criminals,
and more like one of our uplifting videos

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:13.140
about people who, y’know, aren’t Nazi
criminals.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.850
The trouble is, Schindler was a morally complex
guy.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.730
And the picture is about to become even murkier.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:24.639
In November, 1938, the Abwehr ordered Schindler
and Emilie to Ostrava, a grimy industrial

00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:25.741
city on the Polish border.

00:11:25.741 --> 00:11:29.780
For Schindler, this meant numerous business
trips into Poland, gathering intelligence.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:34.420
Yet it also meant doing things that don’t
neatly fit in with the narrative of Schindler

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:35.440
the spy.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:40.329
For instance, it was in Ostrava that Schindler
started bribing local Nazi officials, acting

00:11:40.329 --> 00:11:45.399
less like a true believer than a guy trying
to secure favorable treatment.

00:11:45.399 --> 00:11:49.110
It was also in Ostrava that Schindler began
dabbling in the black market.

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:53.500
Next summer, just a few short months after
Hitler invaded Czechoslovakia, Schindler landed

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.620
a juicy contract to supply stolen Polish army
uniforms to a Nazi agent.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:01.541
Not long after, men dressed as Polish soldiers
launched surprise “attacks” on German

00:12:01.541 --> 00:12:02.610
buildings.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.589
In the aftermath, Hitler declared war on Poland.

00:12:05.589 --> 00:12:08.010
The invasion of Poland lasted barely a month.

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.810
On September 1, 1939, the first tanks rolled
in.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.710
By October 6, the last Polish units had surrendered.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.380
By then, some 60,000 Poles had been killed.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:20.490
From Oskar Schindler’s perspective, though,
the occupation of Poland was simply a whole

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:21.970
new market opening up.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:27.040
Barely had the last fires of resistance been
put out than Schindler was on a train to Krakow,

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.880
eager to help himself to the spoils of war.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.339
In the grand, redbrick city, he took over
a vast apartment that had once belonged to

00:12:33.339 --> 00:12:34.709
a wealthy Jewish family.

00:12:34.709 --> 00:12:38.339
He moved into the black market, began chasing
shady dealings.

00:12:38.339 --> 00:12:40.810
Pretty soon, he was making a killing.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.459
While Emilie was laid up back home with a
debilitating illness, Schindler began living

00:12:44.459 --> 00:12:49.420
the high life, wining and dining local Nazi
leaders, acting like a total playboy.

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:54.500
It’s here that the movie Schindler’s List
opens, with Schindler establishing connections

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:59.529
on the Krakow business scene, not giving the
smallest damn about the suffering around him.

00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:04.110
But if Krakow life was the high water mark
for Schindler the uncaring, money-obsessed

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:06.579
collaborator, it was also something else.

00:13:06.579 --> 00:13:17.540
It was in Krakow that Schindler would finally
discover his own humanity.

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:19.580
The Factory
On November 13, 1939, Schindler took over

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:23.920
a Krakow enamel factory that had once been
owned by Jewish businessmen.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:28.440
Barely had the doors opened in January, 1940,
than Schindler had used his new Nazi contacts

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.540
to land himself a fat contract supplying the
army with kitchenware.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.480
He’d also done something very interesting.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:38.480
He’d rehired one of the Jews who used to
own the factory as his manager, a guy called

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:39.670
Abraham Bankier.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:43.029
Still, Bankier was a rarity in Schindler’s
factory at first.

00:13:43.029 --> 00:13:47.270
By April, 1940, Schindler’s staff had grown
to several hundred, but only 7 of them were

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:48.270
Jewish.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:50.949
The remaining Jews in Krakow were subject
to some strict laws.

00:13:50.949 --> 00:13:55.750
Among restrictions on their clothing and movements,
the Jews also had their wages set by the state,

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.660
at a pitifully low amount.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:02.380
As Schindler’s factory business grew, Abraham
Bankier came to him with an idea.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.089
Why not hire more Jews?

00:14:04.089 --> 00:14:08.040
Thanks to the labor laws, it would be much
cheaper than hiring Poles, thereby saving

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.829
Schindler a pile of money.

00:14:09.829 --> 00:14:12.199
That word, “money”, was the key.

00:14:12.199 --> 00:14:17.279
As his eyeballs presumably transformed into
gigantic dollar signs, Schindler agreed to

00:14:17.279 --> 00:14:19.160
expand his Jewish workforce.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:22.790
It was a decision that would alter the fates
of hundreds of people.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:27.720
By spring, 1941, most of Krakow’s Jews were
living in a ghetto while Schindler continued

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.130
his party lifestyle.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:33.459
It was against this backdrop that the SS announced
Jews could no longer earn wages.

00:14:33.459 --> 00:14:38.290
Employers such as Schindler would have to
pay Jewish wages to the SS instead.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:43.320
It was a minor legal tweak, just another in
a long list of injustice and oppression.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.160
It also changed everything.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:49.860
Suddenly, the ability of Schindler’s Jewish
workers to survive was contingent upon him.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:54.220
He could choose to work them till they dropped
then ask the SS for replacements, or he could

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.190
start feeding them out of his own money.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:15:00.350
Not long after, Schindler arranged for his
black market contacts to start supplying him

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:01.970
extra rations of food.

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:05.949
Come January, 1942, Schindler’s factory
had been converted from somewhere producing

00:15:05.949 --> 00:15:09.450
enamel to somewhere producing armaments for
the German war effort.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:11.730
It’s employees had changed, too.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:16.700
From a mere seven, Schindler was now employing
hundreds of Jews.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:22.350
He Who Saves One Life…

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:26.430
If the last couple of years had allowed Schindler
to be ambivalent about the Nazis, to avoid

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:31.630
taking any one side, 1942 would be the year
that everyone had to choose.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:36.769
On February 15 that year, the first Jews earmarked
for extermination arrived in Auschwitz and

00:15:36.769 --> 00:15:37.990
were immediately gassed.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:42.550
Barely a month later, on March 17, the Belzec
extermination camp opened.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:47.540
Although Auschwitz was closer, it was to Belzec
that the Nazis initially herded Krakow’s

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:48.540
Jews.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.139
It wasn’t quite a mass slaughter yet.

00:15:50.139 --> 00:15:53.980
Those who worked in factories like Schindler’s
were saved from deportation.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:56.690
But things would quickly get much, much worse.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:16:02.170
That June, 1942, the Plaszow work camp opened
4km outside Krakow, atop the remains of two

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.420
Jewish cemeteries.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:07.389
Many of the Jews in Schindler’s employ were
relocated there and forced to march to and

00:16:07.389 --> 00:16:10.399
from the factory every morning and evening.

00:16:10.399 --> 00:16:13.029
It was a grueling existence, and a precarious
one.

00:16:13.029 --> 00:16:17.450
With the SS looking after the camp, any Jew
who stepped out of line could be murdered,

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:18.750
no matter who they worked for.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:22.709
For his part, Schindler did what he could
to make factory life comfortable for his workers.

00:16:22.709 --> 00:16:26.339
But he had no control over what happened in
Plaszow.

00:16:26.339 --> 00:16:28.440
That was about to become a major problem.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:33.570
As a summer in Hell gave way to a winter of
misery, an intellectual young man from a wealthy

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:37.930
Austrian publishing family was dispatched
to Plaszow to take over the camp.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:39.310
His name was Amon Göth.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:43.699
Of all the Nazis we’ve encountered in today’s
story, he was by far the worst.

00:16:43.699 --> 00:16:47.560
Known today for being played by Ralph Fiennes
in Schindler’s List, the real Göth was

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.920
even worse than his fictional counterpart.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.089
As in the film, he used Jews in Plaszow as
target practice.

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:56.279
He trained his dogs to maul Jews to death
on command.

00:16:56.279 --> 00:17:00.339
He hated the idea that any Jew should have
even the faintest taste of freedom.

00:17:00.339 --> 00:17:03.180
So what did Oskar Schindler do when faced
with this monster?

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.470
He set about becoming his very best friend.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:09.780
Over the next few weeks, Schindler showered
Göth in cognac, fine cigars, and cuts of

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.780
meat only available on the black market.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:16.020
He took the sadist to parties, charmed him
with witty conversation, did everything he

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:19.780
could to make sure Göth thought he was a
brownnoser of the highest order.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:24.060
It was only when Göth announced in March
that the Krakow ghetto was to be liquidated

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:28.180
and all the Jews relocated inside Plaszow
that Schindler struck.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:32.850
As SS soldiers wreaked havoc in the ghetto
and the chimneys of Belzec pumped human ash

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:37.320
into the sky, Schindler deployed a charm offensive
as deadly as any weapon.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:41.530
He flattered Göth, cajoled him, bribed him,
offered to let him in on his black market

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:42.530
racket.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:47.070
Before long, he’d wrung an incredible promise
out of this creature in human skin.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:50.940
Göth had agreed to let Schindler relocate
his factory inside Plaszow.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:55.800
What’s more, Schindler would have complete
autonomy in the subcamp to do whatever he

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.800
wanted.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:59.890
For Schindler, that meant the autonomy to
be a decent human being.

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:03.680
The Plaszow Jews who were relocated to Schindler’s
subcamp were given adequate food.

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:05.950
Their living conditions were humane.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:07.680
They were free from violence.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.850
Schindler even banned the SS from entering.

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:14.840
Instead, the Aryan ‘supermen’ were stuck
stewing in towers, watching over Schindler’s

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.760
Jews, but unable to harm them.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:21.420
It was the sort of thing only a man close
to commandant Göth could’ve gotten away

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:22.420
with.

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:25.260
It’s here that we get to the greatest paradox
of Oskar Schindler.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:28.720
Had he been a good man from the start, Schindler
would’ve never been put in a position where

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.370
he could save so many lives.

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:34.580
The Nazis would have confiscated everything
he owned and run him outta town.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:39.180
But because he started out spying against
the Czechs; because he really was a venal,

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:43.830
moneygrubbing, amoral, business-obsessed cynic
at first… he was able to have the trust

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:47.340
of committed Nazis like Göth when he needed
it most.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:52.580
And now, with Plaszow running, and Auschwitz
devouring humans and turning the skies over

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:57.460
Krakow black with burning flesh, Schindler
needed that trust.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:06.630
…Saves the World
In August, 1944, Amon Göth received a disturbing

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:07.630
order.

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:10.460
The Soviet Army was rolling towards Poland
and the Reich was in retreat.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:15.650
Plaszow was to be decommissioned immediately
and all its inhabitants sent to Auschwitz.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:18.660
When Oskar Schindler heard the news, he went
into panic mode.

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:22.650
Despite what they later said in trials and
to themselves as they were trying to get to

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:27.180
sleep at night, everyone in Plaszow knew by
then what Auschwitz was.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:31.270
With time running out, Schindler began loudly
petitioning to have his factory relocated

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:32.840
to Brünnlitz, near his old home.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.680
He was doing essential work, he thundered.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.430
The war effort would fail without him.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:38.240
Hadn’t he always been good to Göth?

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:41.100
Finally, Göth agreed to authorize Schindler’s
transfer.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.080
But what about my workers?

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:44.110
Schindler asked.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.090
Draw up a list, Göth told him.

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:48.890
Any you can prove you need I’ll let you
take with you.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:52.320
And so it was that Schindler’s real-life
list came to be written.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:53.320
However!

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:54.920
It wouldn’t be Schindler who actually wrote
it.

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:59.240
In the movie, Schindler and his accountant
sit down to write the list themselves, agonizing

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:00.570
over every name.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:04.420
In reality, Schindler had to give the job
to a man called Marcel Goldberg.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:05.420
Why?

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:11.580
Because, in September, 1944, Amon Göth was
arrested by the SS for bribery and black marketeering.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.030
He spilled the beans on his co-criminals and
Schindler was arrested too.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.710
That October, while Schindler sat in jail,
Marcel Goldberg prepared the list.

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:22.890
Actually, there were up to nine versions,
all subtly different, and Goldberg’s involvement

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:23.970
was controversial.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:27.760
Some claim he focused on saving the lives
of his friends over those of Schindler’s

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:28.760
workers.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.200
But others paint a picture of Goldberg as
a caring man.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:35.380
One young survivor recalls begging Goldberg
to let her and her mother onto the list.

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.290
Goldberg immediately sat down and added their
names.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:41.820
When the list was finally submitted, the SS
demanded to inspect the workers.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.450
The men were all processed and sent to Brünnlitz.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:47.670
The women, however, were accidentally sent
to Auschwitz.

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.470
From inside his jail, Schindler frantically
arranged a representative to go into the death

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:53.690
camp and save those women from extermination.

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:55.390
It was a close run thing.

00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:56.390
But it worked.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:57.750
The women were returned alive.

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:01.010
Not long after, Schindler’s contacts got
him released from jail.

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:06.320
Finally, after two months of panic, Schindler
and the workers he’d saved arrived in Brünnlitz.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:10.740
Between the bribes he paid, the gifts he showered
on those in power, and the cost of the move

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:15.240
he shouldered, Schindler had by now spent
nearly his entire fortune.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.050
When they arrived, he blew the last of it
on trucks full of wool, leather, shoes, and

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:21.260
clothing materials for his workers.

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:25.620
Schindler and his Jews then settled in to
wait out the war making armaments that were

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.800
designed to fail, doing their own tiny bit
to hinder the Nazi war effort.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:31.300
Amazingly, no-one stopped them.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:34.760
No angry SS officers ever turned up to arrest
Schindler.

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:39.400
When the Third Reich finally collapsed on
May 8, 1945, Schindler had managed to shield

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.980
1,098 Jews from the Holocaust.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:45.850
Now he just had to survive its aftermath.

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:53.620
How a Hero Dies
On May 9, 1945, Oskar Schindler gathered his

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:55.750
employees at his factory for one final goodbye.

00:21:55.750 --> 00:22:00.380
He made a short speech, saying how proud he
was to have helped saved so many Jewish lives.

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:05.020
Then he, Emilie, and a handful of his workers
fled the Sudetenland, barely ahead of the

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:06.620
advancing Soviet Army.

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:10.400
His fortune gone, Schindler would spend the
rest of his life in financial misery.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:15.110
At first, he and Emilie tried settling down
in West Germany, before deciding the US might

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:16.110
be a better bet.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:19.890
Unfortunately, his Nazi party membership automatically
excluded him from America.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:24.880
So Schindler instead submitted an expenses
claim for $1m to the Allies, asking to be

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:28.240
paid back the money he spent saving Jews in
the war.

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:32.670
After a long deliberation, they sent him $15,000
and told him to be happy.

00:22:32.670 --> 00:22:34.530
Schindler spent it on a ticket to Argentina.

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:36.560
It could have been the start of a new life.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:42.040
But while actual Nazi war criminals were able
to start afresh in Argentina and lead peaceful

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.500
lives, Schindler seemed destined to failure.

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:47.090
By 1957, he was forced to declare bankruptcy.

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:50.990
The next year, he abandoned Emilie and caught
a boat back to West Germany.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:54.310
There, he sank into a mire of poverty.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:57.220
And that’s really it, unfortunately.

00:22:57.220 --> 00:22:59.300
Schindler never again enjoyed the highlife.

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.340
Never again got to be the playboy.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:04.080
Never again stood at the center of history.

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:05.810
But he wasn’t totally forgotten.

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:09.310
For the rest of his life, Schindler regularly
received donations.

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:10.550
Not a huge amount.

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:13.140
Just enough to keep him going, keep him safe.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:14.550
Keep him alive.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:19.810
As you’ve probably guessed, those donations
came from the 1,098 Jews he’d saved, a token

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:21.170
of their thanks.

00:23:21.170 --> 00:23:24.340
Known as the Schindlerjuden, they supported
him till the very end.

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.660
That end came on October 9, 1974.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:31.340
By then, Schindler had lived long enough to
see his name added to the Avenue of the Righteous

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:35.060
in Israel by Yad Vashem, a place reserved
for those gentiles who risked their lives

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:37.040
to save Jews during the Holocaust.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:39.880
And it was back to Israel that Schindler returned
in death.

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:42.860
His body was buried in the Catholic Cemetery
on Mount Zion.

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:48.010
Today, it’s estimated there are over 8,500
people alive because of Schindler, descendants

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:49.520
of the Jews he saved.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:53.200
But there are better reasons to remember Schindler
than just for saving lives.

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:57.730
The famous quote goes that “the only thing
necessary for the triumph of evil is for good

00:23:57.730 --> 00:23:59.500
men to do nothing.”

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:04.530
Certainly, plenty of good men alive during
the Holocaust were content to not do anything.

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:07.780
But the life of Oskar Schindler shows that
sometimes the opposite can happen.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:13.740
Sometimes, a man who appears to be amoral,
who appears to just be another uncaring bystander,

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:19.180
can shake themselves out of their stupor to
do a good deed that will change the world.

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:23.160
The quote everyone remembers from Schindler’s
List is the one that goes “he who saves

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.750
one life, saves the entire world.”

00:24:25.750 --> 00:24:30.750
In choosing to be a good man when the downtrodden
needed him most, Schindler helped save the

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:32.170
world a thousand times over.

